      "The internal Fed debate stayed visibly split into the weekend: Bloomberg reported on 7 August that the divide over further tightening has deepened after five years of above-target inflation, with Governor Lisa Cook again saying she is ready to raise rates if disinflation doesn't resume even as New York Fed president John Williams said the current stance remains 'well positioned'. The same day's New York Fed Survey of Consumer Expectations offered a modestly reassuring cross-check: households' perceived probability of finding a new job, if unemployed, rose to 46.2% — the best reading of the year — alongside softer year-ahead inflation expectations, even as the geopolitical backdrop stayed live: Saudi Arabia, Turkey and Pakistan signed a NATO-style 'Mecca Joint Defence Pact' the same day as Trump said the Iran war 'can't go much longer', with Tehran and Oman still working through the final terms of a Strait of Hormuz shipping framework.",
