    bottomLine: "The FOMC held at 3.50–3.75% on 29 July, but three members dissented for a hike — the first three-way hawkish hold-dissent since September 2016. That hawkish tilt was cut sharply back on 7 August when nonfarm payrolls unexpectedly fell 23,000 in July (vs +83,000 consensus) with 103,000 of downward May/June revisions; CME FedWatch-implied odds of a 16 September hike collapsed to roughly 44% (from 81% on 30 July), even as the unemployment rate's dip to 4.1% reflected a shrinking labour force rather than stronger hiring. Equities read the report as dovish, with the S&P 500 (7,757.64), Nasdaq Composite (26,690.62) and Dow (54,036.93) all closing at or near fresh records on a second straight winning week. Capital Economics' Thomas Ryan said the miss would revive Fed concern about the labour market, while Bank of America held its call for a September hike, arguing the Fed remains more focused on inflation — leaving 12 August's CPI print as the next swing factor for the 16 September decision.",
    // Market-implied odds for the next FOMC decision (CME FedWatch) and the FOMC's
