    bottomLine: "The MPC held at 3.75% on 30 July but the vote split hardened to 6–3 (Greene, Mann and Pill dissenting for an immediate 25bp hike) — one more dissenter than June's 7–2 and a narrower hold than the 7–2 most economists expected. The Bank's own projections show CPI peaking near 3.2% in Q4 2026; markets continue to price a 25bp hike arriving around the November or December MPC meeting, taking Bank Rate toward ~4.20% by end-2026 and a ~4.20–4.25% peak in early 2027, though Berenberg and Oxford Economics see that as an overshoot and expect a hold well into 2027. The FTSE 100 touched a fresh intraday record (10,979.60) on the decision day but closed down 11.14 points (-0.1%) at 10,897.27 as early gains faded after Bailey's press conference, even with Rolls-Royce up 6.0% on strong earnings; Rentokil Initial slumped 21% on weak North American demand, and sterling eased slightly off its intraday high to trade near $1.338. Burnham/Healey's VAT cut on household electricity (from October) trims CPI by an estimated 0.10pp, a small disinflationary offset alongside their earlier 20% business-rates cut for pubs, clubs and music venues from April 2027.",
